Yes. More generally:

\boxed{\text{Ratios are one of the fundamental ways probability is encoded.}}

Probability itself is a normalized ratio:

P(E)=\frac{\text{favorable outcomes}}{\text{possible outcomes}}

But the idea extends much further.

Odds

Instead of probability:

P=\frac{a}{a+b}

you can encode the same information as odds:

O=\frac{a}{b}

where

* a = evidence for,
* b = evidence against.

You can convert between them:

P=\frac{O}{1+O}

Bayesian view

Evidence changes ratios more naturally than absolute probabilities.

Posterior odds are:

\text{Posterior Odds}
=
\text{Prior Odds}
\times
\text{Likelihood Ratio}

This is one reason likelihood ratios are so important in statistics and diagnostics.

Information geometry

Every observation changes the ratios between competing hypotheses.

Suppose:

H=\{\text{random},\text{providence},\text{AI},\text{emergence}\}

The notification changes the relative ratios:

1:1:1:1
\quad\rightarrow\quad
1:2:1.3:1.4

Those numbers are illustrative only, but the point is that learning is fundamentally about updating relative weights.

Your topological idea

You can go one step further.

Instead of treating probability as a scalar, think of it as curvature on a landscape.

Ratios define gradients.

Gradients define flow.

Flow defines behavior.

Symbolically:

\text{Ratios}
\rightarrow
\text{Probabilities}
\rightarrow
\text{Potential Field}
\rightarrow
\text{Trajectory}

Now the question changes from:

“What is the probability?”

to

“Where does the landscape naturally guide a rational agent?”

That’s close to your earlier mechanism-invariant idea. Different mechanisms can produce similar landscapes, and if those landscapes have the same topology, they induce similar optimal behaviors.

In that sense, ratios aren’t just encoding probability—they’re encoding the relative structure of possibilities. Probability is one interpretation of that structure; optimization, inference, and decision-making are others.